 "content": {
  "subject": "Electrical Machines",
  "topic": "DC Motor Back EMF and Speed Regulation",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "hook": "Weaken the magnetic field of a DC motor, and it actually speeds up!",
  "question": "A DC shunt motor drives a constant torque load. If the field flux is halved while the terminal voltage is kept constant, what happens to the armature current?",
  "options": [
   "It remains unchanged",
   "It is halved",
   "It is doubled",
   "It quadruples"
  ],
  "correctIndex": 2,
  "answerLine": "The armature current is doubled.",
  "explanation": [
   "In any DC machine, electromagnetic torque is directly proportional to both magnetic flux and armature current.",
   "Because the mechanical load torque remains strictly constant, the product of flux and current must stay constant.",
   "When you cut the field flux by half, the motor shaft momentarily decelerates due to torque deficit.",
   "Back EMF is directly proportional to flux times speed, so back EMF drops instantly by half."
  ],
  "funFact": "DC motors run dangerously fast if their field circuit opens accidentally, a condition called runaway.",
  "outro": "Master your electrical machine equations every day, and subscribe for more JE exam shortcuts!",
