  "fingerprint": "A DC shunt motor drives a constant torque load. If the field flux is halved while the terminal voltage is kept constant, what happens to the armature current?~~It remains unchanged|It is halved|It is doubled|It quadruples~~2~~In any DC machine, electromagnetic torque is directly proportional to both magnetic flux and armature current.|Because the mechanical load torque remains strictly constant, the product of flux and current must stay constant.|When you cut the field flux by half, the motor shaft momentarily decelerates due to torque deficit.|Back EMF is directly proportional to flux times speed, so back EMF drops instantly by half.~~In any DC machine, electromagnetic torque is directly proportional to both magnetic flux and armature current.|Because the mechanical load torque remains strictly constant, the product of flux and current must stay constant.|When you cut the field flux by half, the motor shaft momentarily decelerates due to torque deficit.|Back EMF is directly proportional to flux times speed, so back EMF drops instantly by half.|With supply voltage fixed, the armature voltage drop equals supply voltage minus this lower back EMF value.|That sudden voltage imbalance forces the armature current to surge upwards until mechanical equilibrium is restored.|To produce the exact original torque with half the flux, the armature current must exactly double.|Speed then climbs higher until the newly established back EMF satisfies the steady armature current demand.|Therefore, under constant load torque, field flux weakening always forces armature current to increase inversely."
